Hello Hackaday,

a tip where the implementation detail may be the interesting part.

Solidon3D recognises patterns in downloaded meshes, honeycombs, knurls, ribs,
and presents them as one editable feature with pitch, cell width and depth
rather than as the few hundred triangles they actually are. Remove with a
click, reapply with three numbers.

The part that took the work is the cylinder. A pattern wrapped around a handle
cannot be measured in the plane: the grooves would be deeper at the crest than
in the valley, and the last cell would not meet the first. Solidon measures the
pattern in the unrolled surface, fills the cells on the facets themselves and
redraws it there, so the grooves are equally deep everywhere. The pitch is
moved to a divisor of the circumference so the pattern closes without a seam,
and the software names the pitch it chose instead of silently rounding.

The second item, for the CAD-minded: as of this release two geometry kernels
run under one interface with no user-visible switch. A downloaded mesh becomes
an exact body where that is possible, holes and threads stay real faces and
edges through moving, duplicating and removing, and STEP round-trips cleanly.
Where the exact body cannot be kept, the software says so rather than
converting quietly.

A third, smaller one: an imported thread is measured, so pitch, number of
starts, handedness and nominal diameter, and can have its counterpart
generated on the mating part in a table size, as a fit. It refuses when no
table size matches instead of rounding 6.4 x 1.1 into an M6.

And one that tends to surprise people: clearance is not a number in this
program. You pick "clearance for PETG" and the value comes from the material
profile, which you can calibrate to your own printer by printing the supplied
tolerance ladder and measuring it. Change the material and every fit in the
project follows.

Solidon3D runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, one-time purchase, fully offline
after a single activation, no account, no telemetry. The demo is free and
complete.

